      "roundness": null,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "Terraform for PostgreSQL\nThe PostgreSQL provider lets\nTerraform manage the objects\ninside a PostgreSQL server: roles,\ndatabases, schemas, grants,\ndefault privileges and extensions.\nA community maintainer publishes\nit under the source address\n`cyrilgdn/postgresql` ([provider d\nocs](https://registry.terraform.io\n/providers/cyrilgdn/postgresql/lat\nest/docs)). It replaced\nHashiCorp's older provider ([READM\nE](https://github.com/cyrilgdn/ter\nraform-provider-postgresql/blob/v1\n.27.0/README.md)). The [Terraform\nmap](../terraform.md) explains\nTerraform itself.\n\nThe provider is a SQL client, not\nan API client. It opens a database\nconnection as an admin role and\nruns SQL such as `CREATE ROLE` and\n`GRANT`. Even a refresh queries\nthe system catalogs, so every plan\nneeds a live connection to the\nserver ([A SQL Client](#a-sql-clie\nnt-a-sql-client)).\n\nThe unit is one server and, by\ndefault, one database. A cloud\nprovider can run the server, for\nexample Amazon RDS (Relational\nDatabase Service). Then two\nproviders share the work ([Two\nPlanes, Two Credentials](#two-plan\nes-two-credentials-two-planes-two-\ncredentials)):\n\n- The AWS provider creates the RDS\ninstance, through the AWS API.\n- The PostgreSQL provider creates\nwhat lives inside the instance,\nthrough a database connection.\n\nInside the server, access follows\nthree rules that shape every plan:\n\n- A grant resource owns the full\nprivilege set of one role on one\nkind of object in one schema ([A\nGrant Owns Its Set](#a-grant-owns-\nits-set-a-grant-owns-its-set)).\n- The owner of an object has all\nprivileges on it without any grant\n([Owners Need No Grants](#owners-n\need-no-grants-owners-need-no-grant\ns)).\n- Default privileges apply to\nobjects that one named role\ncreates later ([Default Privileges\nFollow the Creator](#default-privi\nleges-follow-the-creator-default-p\nrivileges-follow-the-creator)).\n\nOn RDS, the admin role is not a\nfull superuser, which limits what\nthe provider can do ([The RDS\nAdmin Is Not a Superuser](#the-rds\n-admin-is-not-a-superuser-the-rds-\nadmin-is-not-a-superuser)).\n\nNow compare Snowflake. A Snowflake\nfuture grant and a PostgreSQL\ndefault privilege both cover\nobjects that do not exist yet.\nSnowflake keys the rule on a\ndatabase or schema; PostgreSQL\nkeys it on the role that creates\nthe objects. Default Privileges\nFollow the Creator compares the\ntwo.\n\nThe map has four parts. Mental\nModels explains the ideas above.\nReading a PostgreSQL Configuration\nsays what you will see and where\nto look first. Pitfalls lists the\nfailures that experienced users\nplan around. Good and Bad\nDecisions compares the main design\nchoices.",
      "fontSize": 18,
      "fontFamily": 2,
      "textAlign": "center",
      "verticalAlign": "middle",
      "baseline": 22.5,
      "containerId": "node-719ebb249997",
      "originalText": "Terraform for PostgreSQL\nThe PostgreSQL provider lets Terraform manage the objects inside a PostgreSQL server: roles, databases, schemas, grants, default privileges and extensions. A community maintainer publishes it under the source address `cyrilgdn/postgresql` ([provider docs](https://registry.terraform.io/providers/cyrilgdn/postgresql/latest/docs)). It replaced HashiCorp's older provider ([README](https://github.com/cyrilgdn/terraform-provider-postgresql/blob/v1.27.0/README.md)). The [Terraform map](../terraform.md) explains Terraform itself.\n\nThe provider is a SQL client, not an API client. It opens a database connection as an admin role and runs SQL such as `CREATE ROLE` and `GRANT`. Even a refresh queries the system catalogs, so every plan needs a live connection to the server ([A SQL Client](#a-sql-client-a-sql-client)).\n\nThe unit is one server and, by default, one database. A cloud provider can run the server, for example Amazon RDS (Relational Database Service). Then two providers share the work ([Two Planes, Two Credentials](#two-planes-two-credentials-two-planes-two-credentials)):\n\n- The AWS provider creates the RDS instance, through the AWS API.\n- The PostgreSQL provider creates what lives inside the instance, through a database connection.\n\nInside the server, access follows three rules that shape every plan:\n\n- A grant resource owns the full privilege set of one role on one kind of object in one schema ([A Grant Owns Its Set](#a-grant-owns-its-set-a-grant-owns-its-set)).\n- The owner of an object has all privileges on it without any grant ([Owners Need No Grants](#owners-need-no-grants-owners-need-no-grants)).\n- Default privileges apply to objects that one named role creates later ([Default Privileges Follow the Creator](#default-privileges-follow-the-creator-default-privileges-follow-the-creator)).\n\nOn RDS, the admin role is not a full superuser, which limits what the provider can do ([The RDS Admin Is Not a Superuser](#the-rds-admin-is-not-a-superuser-the-rds-admin-is-not-a-superuser)).\n\nNow compare Snowflake. A Snowflake future grant and a PostgreSQL default privilege both cover objects that do not exist yet. Snowflake keys the rule on a database or schema; PostgreSQL keys it on the role that creates the objects. Default Privileges Follow the Creator compares the two.\n\nThe map has four parts. Mental Models explains the ideas above. Reading a PostgreSQL Configuration says what you will see and where to look first. Pitfalls lists the failures that experienced users plan around. Good and Bad Decisions compares the main design choices.",
      "lineHeight": 1.25,
      "autoResize": true,
      "customData": {
        "node_id": "terraform-postgresql"
      }
    },
