      }
    },
    {
      "type": "text",
      "version": 1,
      "versionNonce": 7900768750,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "id": "text-7bbe586948d3",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 1,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "angle": 0,
      "x": 1288.8,
      "y": 1160.0,
      "strokeColor": "#1E3A8A",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "width": 394.8,
      "height": 577.5,
      "seed": 264746,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "roundness": null,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "Default Privileges Follow the Creator\n`postgresql_default_privileges` runs `ALTER\nDEFAULT PRIVILEGES FOR ROLE <owner>`. It\napplies only to objects that the `owner` role\ncreates after the rule exists ([resource doc](\nhttps://github.com/cyrilgdn/terraform-provider\n-postgresql/blob/v1.27.0/website/docs/r/postgr\nesql_default_privileges.html.markdown#L33)).\nPostgreSQL states that new objects are only\naffected by the default privileges of the role\nthat creates them, and that existing objects\ndo not change ([ALTER DEFAULT PRIVILEGES](http\ns://www.postgresql.org/docs/current/sql-alterd\nefaultprivileges.html)).\n\n| | PostgreSQL default privileges | Snowflake\nfuture grants |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Keyed by | The role that creates the objects\n| The database or schema that holds them |\n| Existing objects | Unchanged; add a grant on\nexisting tables | Unchanged; add an `all`\ngrant |\n| When two rules overlap | Schema-level rules\nadd to the global ones | Schema-level rules\nreplace database-level ones |\n\nThe Snowflake column comes from Snowflake's\n[GRANT docs](https://docs.snowflake.com/en/sql\n-reference/sql/grant-privilege); the\n[Snowflake map](../snowflake/terraform-snowfla\nke.md#schema-level-future-grants-win-schema-le\nvel-future-grants-win) explains its trap.",
      "fontSize": 14,
      "fontFamily": 2,
      "textAlign": "center",
      "verticalAlign": "middle",
      "baseline": 17.5,
      "containerId": "node-7bbe586948d3",
      "originalText": "Default Privileges Follow the Creator\n`postgresql_default_privileges` runs `ALTER DEFAULT PRIVILEGES FOR ROLE <owner>`. It applies only to objects that the `owner` role creates after the rule exists ([resource doc](https://github.com/cyrilgdn/terraform-provider-postgresql/blob/v1.27.0/website/docs/r/postgresql_default_privileges.html.markdown#L33)). PostgreSQL states that new objects are only affected by the default privileges of the role that creates them, and that existing objects do not change ([ALTER DEFAULT PRIVILEGES](https://www.postgresql.org/docs/current/sql-alterdefaultprivileges.html)).\n\n| | PostgreSQL default privileges | Snowflake future grants |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Keyed by | The role that creates the objects | The database or schema that holds them |\n| Existing objects | Unchanged; add a grant on existing tables | Unchanged; add an `all` grant |\n| When two rules overlap | Schema-level rules add to the global ones | Schema-level rules replace database-level ones |\n\nThe Snowflake column comes from Snowflake's [GRANT docs](https://docs.snowflake.com/en/sql-reference/sql/grant-privilege); the [Snowflake map](../snowflake/terraform-snowflake.md#schema-level-future-grants-win-schema-level-future-grants-win) explains its trap.",
      "lineHeight": 1.25,
      "autoResize": true,
      "customData": {
        "node_id": "default-privileges-follow-the-creator"
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "rectangle",
      "version": 1,
